<BaseLayout title="What Is Singulation? Singulation Conveyors & Single Filers" description="Singulation is separating a mass of products into single file with a gap between each unit. How singulators, single filers, and speed-differential belts do it." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/singulation/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Singulation Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Singulation separates bulk or mass-flow product streams into individual, spaced units for scanning, labeling, diverting, or machine feeding. Products enter the singulation zone touching, overlapping, or side-by-side and exit in single file with a controlled gap between each unit. Singulation conveyor systems serve e-commerce distribution, beverage packaging, food processing, and pharmaceutical serialization lines where downstream operations require individual product access.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">What Is Singulation?</h2>
<p>Singulation is the conveyor function that separates a mass of touching, overlapping, or side-by-side products into single file with a gap between each unit. Equipment built for it is called a singulator, a singulation conveyor, or a single filer (single filing conveyor).</p>
<p>Downstream processes need individual product access. Barcode scanners read one label at a time. Checkweighers weigh one package at a time. Diverters push one product at a time. Labelers apply one label at a time. None of these operations work when products arrive in a mass, touching, overlapping, stacked, or side-by-side. Singulation creates the conditions these downstream systems require: single file, one product at a time, with a gap between each unit. In pharmaceutical packaging, the FDA's <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/drug-supply-chain-security-act-dscsa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Drug Supply Chain Security Act</a> calls for prescription drugs to be identified and traced at the package level, so each unit must pass the serialization scanner on its own.</p>
<p>Without singulation, automated scanning and sorting systems produce read errors, double-reads, and mis-sorts. A barcode scanner aimed at a conveyor of touching parcels reads the barcode on the leading parcel but may also partially read the barcode on the parcel behind it, creating a double-read that confuses the sortation controller. A diverter timed to push one parcel pushes two touching parcels together, sending the wrong parcel down the wrong lane. Singulation eliminates these failures by guaranteeing a gap between every product.</p>
<p>Singulation is distinct from <a href="/conveyor-functions/metering/">metering</a>. Singulation separates touching products into individuals. Metering controls the rate and precise spacing of already-separated products. In practice, most packaging lines perform singulation first (create individuals from a mass) and metering second (control the spacing and rate of those individuals entering the next machine). The two functions often occur on adjacent conveyor sections and are sometimes combined in a single system.</p>
