<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is singulation on a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Singulation separates a bulk or mass-flow product stream into individual, spaced units in single file. Products enter touching or overlapping and exit with a controlled gap between each unit. Singulation prepares products for scanning, labeling, diverting, or machine feeding.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How does speed-differential singulation work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Two or more belts run at progressively faster speeds. Products transfer from a slower belt to a faster one. The speed increase pulls the leading product away from trailing products, creating a gap. Multiple speed zones in sequence widen the gap until each product is individually separated.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between singulation and metering?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/singulation/">Singulation</a> separates touching products into individuals. <a href="/conveyor-functions/metering/">Metering</a> controls the rate and spacing of already-separated products. Singulation happens first (mass to single file), metering happens second (controlling the feed rate into a machine).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What industries use singulation conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>E-commerce and parcel distribution (package scanning and sorting), <a href="/industries/warehouse-conveyors/">warehouse operations</a>, beverage lines (bottle singulation for labeling and packing), food packaging (individual package scanning), and pharmaceutical lines (serialization scanning requiring individual unit identification).</p>

</div>
